1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,791
Ten minutes past eleven, on the morning of the fifth of November 2026.

2
00:00:05,791 --> 00:00:09,372
Dr APJ Abdul Kalam Island, off the coast of Odisha.

3
00:00:09,372 --> 00:00:13,023
A sealed tube swings upright on the back of a truck.

4
00:00:13,023 --> 00:00:13,781
Then fire.

5
00:00:13,781 --> 00:00:18,351
The missile climbs, flattens out, turns, and hits its target.

6
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:25,978
Hours later, the Defence Ministry said its induction "has become imminent".

7
00:00:25,978 --> 00:00:30,469
This is Pralay, and it may be the most important missile India has built in years.

8
00:00:30,469 --> 00:00:31,688
Let's decode it.

9
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:37,595
First, the spec sheet.

10
00:00:37,595 --> 00:00:39,427
Weight: about five tonnes.

11
00:00:39,427 --> 00:00:40,310
Fuel: solid.

12
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:44,179
Range: one hundred and fifty to five hundred kilometres.

13
00:00:44,179 --> 00:00:52,285
Warhead: conventional, five hundred to one thousand kilograms, and the heavier the warhead, the shorter the reach.

14
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:58,495
Home: a sealed canister on an eight-by-eight road-mobile launcher.

15
00:00:58,495 --> 00:01:05,562
Designed by DRDO's Research Centre Imarat in Hyderabad, with Bharat Dynamics Limited as its production partner.

16
00:01:05,562 --> 00:01:06,446
And the name?

17
00:01:06,446 --> 00:01:10,737
In Sanskrit, pralay means the great dissolution, the end of an age.

18
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:12,236
Not a subtle name.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,868
To picture five hundred kilometres, start in Hyderabad, where Pralay was designed.

20
00:01:22,868 --> 00:01:27,737
A straight line from there to Bengaluru is almost exactly five hundred kilometres.

21
00:01:27,737 --> 00:01:30,200
An airliner needs about an hour for that.

22
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,790
Pralay needs minutes.

23
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,523
Here's what makes Pralay special.

24
00:01:36,523 --> 00:01:39,708
There are three ways to fly a missile to a target.

25
00:01:39,708 --> 00:01:40,707
One: ballistic.

26
00:01:40,707 --> 00:01:43,517
Throw it high, let it fall along a tall arc.

27
00:01:43,517 --> 00:01:45,266
It's fast, but predictable.

28
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:52,685
Once a radar sees that arc, a defence system can calculate where it will land and send an interceptor to meet it.

29
00:01:57,833 --> 00:01:58,687
Two: cruise.

30
00:01:58,687 --> 00:02:02,104
It flies low, like a small jet, hugging the ground.

31
00:02:02,104 --> 00:02:04,469
It's hard to spot, but much slower.

32
00:02:04,469 --> 00:02:07,426
And three: quasi-ballistic, which is Pralay.

33
00:02:07,426 --> 00:02:15,282
It launches like a ballistic missile, but flies a lower, flatter arc, and in the final stage it can change course.

34
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:22,962
The radar's prediction says it lands here, and then it doesn't.

35
00:02:22,962 --> 00:02:27,087
Defence sources told ThePrint it was designed to evade interceptor missiles,

36
00:02:27,087 --> 00:02:34,352
and a defence scientist quoted after the latest trial said it flies at hypersonic speed, more than five times the speed of sound.

37
00:02:40,733 --> 00:02:42,235
Now look at the launcher.

38
00:02:42,235 --> 00:02:49,053
Older missiles that ran on liquid fuel had to be fuelled before firing, which took time and could give the game away.

39
00:02:49,053 --> 00:02:52,993
Pralay's solid fuel is already inside, sealed in its canister.

40
00:02:56,033 --> 00:03:00,279
The truck drives in, stops, raises the tube, fires, and moves on.

41
00:03:00,279 --> 00:03:02,467
Soldiers call it shoot and scoot.

42
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:08,685
A launcher that never stays in one place is far harder to find, and far harder to hit back.

43
00:03:13,667 --> 00:03:15,657
Pralay didn't come from nowhere.

44
00:03:15,657 --> 00:03:20,965
India's first ballistic missile, Prithvi, joined the forces more than thirty years ago.

45
00:03:20,965 --> 00:03:27,539
In 2011, DRDO first tested a smaller tactical missile called Prahaar, and Pralay grew out of that programme.

46
00:03:27,539 --> 00:03:34,112
The project was sanctioned in 2015, with a budget of about three hundred and thirty-three crore rupees.

47
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:41,572
The first two test flights came on the twenty-second and twenty-third of December 2021.

48
00:03:41,572 --> 00:03:48,834
Meanwhile, the clashes with China along the Line of Actual Control in mid-2020 had changed the Army's thinking,

49
00:03:48,834 --> 00:03:50,973
and in 2022 it asked for Pralay.

50
00:03:50,973 --> 00:03:54,839
Another test followed on the seventh of November 2023.

51
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:03,341
On the twenty-sixth of January 2025, Pralay rolled down Kartavya Path in the Republic Day parade.

52
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:10,431
On the twenty-eighth and twenty-ninth of July 2025, two back-to-back shots checked its longest and shortest range.

53
00:04:10,431 --> 00:04:18,013
And on the fifth of November 2026 came the user trial, where the armed forces themselves put the system through its paces.

54
00:04:18,013 --> 00:04:22,875
Reports say orders have been cleared for both the Army and the Air Force.

55
00:04:24,633 --> 00:04:26,295
So why does India need it?

56
00:04:26,295 --> 00:04:27,956
Picture a ladder of reach.

57
00:04:27,956 --> 00:04:32,817
At the bottom: artillery and rockets like Pinaka, reaching tens of kilometres.

58
00:04:32,817 --> 00:04:40,570
At the top: the Agni missiles, reaching from seven hundred to eight thousand kilometres, part of India's strategic deterrent.

59
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:42,355
In between, there was a gap.

60
00:04:42,355 --> 00:04:46,293
Pralay fills it: a conventional missile that can hit airfields,

61
00:04:46,293 --> 00:04:51,084
radar sites and command centres hundreds of kilometres away, in minutes.

62
00:04:57,667 --> 00:04:59,877
And that word, conventional, matters.

63
00:04:59,877 --> 00:05:02,728
DRDO describes Pralay as a conventional missile,

64
00:05:02,728 --> 00:05:07,847
and India's nuclear weapons stay with a separate command, the Strategic Forces Command.

65
00:05:07,847 --> 00:05:11,928
Pralay is meant for the battlefield, not for nuclear deterrence.

66
00:05:16,967 --> 00:05:18,283
How does it compare?

67
00:05:18,283 --> 00:05:22,734
Start at home, with BrahMos, India's famous supersonic cruise missile.

68
00:05:22,734 --> 00:05:25,995
BrahMos skims low and can weave towards its target.

69
00:05:25,995 --> 00:05:28,502
Pralay climbs and dives, and is faster.

70
00:05:28,502 --> 00:05:34,834
Defence sources told ThePrint that Pralay's edge is speed, while BrahMos brings agility and stealth.

71
00:05:34,834 --> 00:05:37,391
They aren't rivals; they're a team.

72
00:05:43,967 --> 00:05:50,492
Abroad, Pralay's closest cousins are Russia's Iskander, used in the war in Ukraine, China's Dong Feng-12,

73
00:05:50,492 --> 00:05:57,202
America's Precision Strike Missile and South Korea's Hyunmoo-2: fast, mobile, accurate battlefield missiles.

74
00:05:57,202 --> 00:06:00,034
And India's neighbours are already investing.

75
00:06:00,034 --> 00:06:04,713
On the thirteenth of January 2026, Army chief General Upendra Dwivedi said,

76
00:06:04,713 --> 00:06:12,216
"Pakistan has established a rocket force," adding, "It is the need of the hour that we also establish such a force."

77
00:06:15,433 --> 00:06:21,009
Which brings us to the fight inside India's defence world: who should control missiles like Pralay?

78
00:06:21,009 --> 00:06:27,888
The idea of a dedicated rocket force goes back to the late General Bipin Rawat, India's first Chief of Defence Staff.

79
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:37,528
Supporters, like retired Brigadier Anil Raman, argue that India's long-range strike weapons are spread across the Army,

80
00:06:37,528 --> 00:06:42,125
the Air Force and the Navy, and that one command would decide faster in a crisis.

81
00:06:42,125 --> 00:06:48,741
Critics, like retired Air Marshal Narmdeshwar Tiwari, point out that only China and Russia have created such a force,

82
00:06:48,741 --> 00:06:52,329
and say India should invest in modern fighter aircraft instead.

83
00:06:52,329 --> 00:06:55,769
The government has not yet announced a final structure.

84
00:06:59,533 --> 00:07:02,754
If you remember one thing, make it three words.

85
00:07:02,754 --> 00:07:06,309
Low: a flatter arc than a classic ballistic missile.

86
00:07:06,309 --> 00:07:09,865
Late: it turns in the final stage, to beat defences.

87
00:07:09,865 --> 00:07:14,392
Loaded: sealed in its canister with solid fuel, ready to fire.

88
00:07:20,067 --> 00:07:20,838
What's next?

89
00:07:20,838 --> 00:07:23,744
The Defence Ministry says induction is imminent.

90
00:07:23,744 --> 00:07:28,905
The big open questions are how many missiles will be built, and who will command them.

91
00:07:28,905 --> 00:07:33,950
As of the thirteenth of November 2026, those decisions have not been announced.

92
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:41,360
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: BrahMos, the missile India built with Russia.

93
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:45,336
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

